## (root)
#c9[i5] Logrus is in maintenance mode, focusing only on security, bug fixes, performance improvements, and interoperability changes such as Go's log/slog.
  > README.md:6-9 #a2e51bd5d0ab
#c10[i4] Logrus provides seven distinct logging levels: Trace, Debug, Info, Warning, Error, Fatal, and Panic.
  > README.md::"Level logging" #6386bc0ba3b9
#c11[i3] Logging with Fatal invokes os.Exit(1), whereas Panic calls panic() after logging.
  > README.md::"Level logging" #6386bc0ba3b9
#c12[i3] Enabling method caller reporting via logrus.SetReportCaller(true) adds the caller as 'method' with a 20 to 40% performance overhead in Go 1.6 and 1.7.
  > README.md::"Logging Method Name" #8f0cdd7719fd
#c13[i3] Registered exit handlers via RegisterExitHandler execute before Logrus invokes os.Exit(1) on fatal-level logs.
  > README.md::"Fatal handlers" #c68021be4d42
#c14[i3] Transforming a Logrus logger into an io.Writer using logger.Writer() outputs each written line at the info level.
  > README.md::"Logger as an `io.Writer`" #43c8882c77fb
#c15[i3] Logger concurrency locking can be disabled with logger.SetNoLock() if hooks and logger.Out writes are already thread-safe.
  > README.md::"Thread safety" #00944f061c80
#c16[i2] Log rotation is deliberately not provided within Logrus and should instead be handled by external programs like logrotate(8).
  > README.md::Rotation #3e30e434ee20
#c17[i4] runHandler catches panics from an exit handler and prints an error message to os.Stderr.
  > alt_exit.go::runHandler.func #6c6e495d0da9
#c18[i5] Exit executes all registered handlers sequentially via runHandlers and then calls os.Exit with the given code.
  > alt_exit.go::Exit #1a8eb5e92b99
#c19[i4] RegisterExitHandler appends an exit handler function to the internal handlers list.
  > alt_exit.go::RegisterExitHandler #21710e085157
#c20[i4] DeferExitHandler prepends an exit handler function to the front of the handlers list.
  > alt_exit.go::DeferExitHandler #ad6fd8ee9de6
#c21[i5] The BufferPool interface defines Put(*bytes.Buffer) and Get() *bytes.Buffer methods for managing buffer reuse.
  > buffer_pool.go::BufferPool #8bcb5a628386
#c22[i4] The default buffer pool implementation wraps a sync.Pool whose New function allocates and returns a new bytes.Buffer.
  > buffer_pool.go:8-14 #bc12a126a85e
#c23[i4] SetBufferPool replaces the package-level default logrus buffer pool with a custom BufferPool implementation.
  > buffer_pool.go::SetBufferPool #b12b7257c166
#c24[i3] defaultPool.Get retrieves an item from its sync.Pool and type-asserts it to *bytes.Buffer.
  > buffer_pool.go::Get #f4f79ce665e5
#c25[i3] defaultPool.Put delegates returning a bytes.Buffer directly to the underlying sync.Pool.
  > buffer_pool.go:25-27 #e37fad26665d
#c26[i5] Logrus is a structured logger for Go that is completely API-compatible with the standard library logger.
  > doc.go:2 #c15b9a63b369
#c27[i3] The simplest usage of Logrus relies on its package-level exported logger.
  > doc.go:4 #a0b77d449162
#c28[i4] Logrus supports structured logging via log.WithFields using log.Fields key-value pairs before calling log level methods like Info.
  > doc.go:13-18 #7acbe495a4bd
#c29[i4] An Entry requires a non-nil Logger reference, and logging methods panic if the entry has a nil Logger.
  > entry.go:44-46 #36ec58c2b314
#c30[i4] Derived Entry methods like WithField, WithFields, WithContext, and Dup create copies of the Entry and clone Data to prevent in-place mutation.
  > entry.go::Dup #41f846b1d1af
#c31[i3] Adding non-error function or pointer-to-function values to Entry.Data is rejected and records an internal error message instead.
  > entry.go::addField #f676bd1af7e1
#c32[i3] When Logger.ReportCaller is enabled and Caller is not already set, logrus scans runtime call stack frames to resolve the first non-logrus caller.
  > entry.go::log #ce98475925b3
#c33[i3] During hook dispatch, if any hook's Fire method returns an error, the error is printed to os.Stderr and subsequent hooks are aborted.
  > entry.go::fireHooks #a8201a48afd7
#c34[i4] Generic Log, Logf, and Logln calls with PanicLevel or FatalLevel do not trigger a panic or call Logger.Exit.
  > entry.go:398-400 #8ee92ce49c7c
#c35[i3] Panic logging methods invoke panic passing the fully populated Entry pointer after hooks and output writes have executed.
  > entry.go::log #ce98475925b3
